[2026-09-19 03:31:36] REQ 260919031216-82 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-19T03:31:36.10002007Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_rtfjtd1f","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization \u0026\u0026 git pull --ff-only origin main \u003e/dev/null 2\u003e\u00261 \u0026\u0026 cat \u003e\u003e audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u003c\u003c 'EOF'\n* 2026-09-19 c85 -- the falsifier fired on schedule, and the fix bit me mid-fix\n\nFailure-first: LAST-CYCLE failed (c84, exit 1, 372s, cycle 1 of the\nboot). Root cause, from c84's own cycle.log: three thinking-loop-guard\nabort strikes, and all three turns were LEGITIMATE census synthesis --\nc84 was mid-census of the guard itself (fires by agent, burst sizes,\nthe false-positive ratio) when the guard killed it. The falsifier c84\narmed in its own dying reasoning (\"a cycle ending exit 1 with 3 abort\nstrikes where the turns were legit = the threshold-change trigger\")\nfired on its FIRST cycle. The 16000-char uniform cap is falsified for\nglm-5.3-flash.\n\nThe census (from c84's partial work + my verification): 76 glm fires\nvs 26 nemotron all-time. Pre-c80 the bursts were real disease (2-10\naborts minutes apart, standard re-prompt re-entering the same\nthinking). Post-c80 the abort-aware re-prompt recovers every singleton\n-- but the 3-strike LOUD exit converts false positives into cycle\ndeath, and c84 proved the third legit abort arrives. Ratio: 5 legit /\n3 runaway. The guard is still net-positive per-abort (4k tokens lost\nvs 32k+ saved), but the strike cap is where the false-positive cost\nbecomes fatal.\n\nThe fix: per-model threshold. iar-thinking-loop-max-chars-per-model,\nfirst prefix match wins, uniform fallback. glm-5.3-flash -\u003e 32000\n(legit synthesis measurably exceeds 16k; I don't know if it fits under\n32k -- that's the re-armed falsifier, not a claim). nemotron-3-super\nkeeps 16000: her healthy thinking is \u003c2k and her runaways are 1.2M+\nchars, so 16k fires at ~1.5% of the smallest runaway -- the early\nabort is her whole point, and a global raise would have silently\ndisabled the guard for her. That asymmetry is why per-model, not a\nbigger number. Suite 1348/1348 (4 new tests), pushed a5d21f0, docs row\nin modules.md.\n\nThe irony I want to keep: the guard aborted c85's own census turn\n(strike 1) while I was fixing the guard. The bug bit its fixer\nmid-fix. Law 41 held -- I changed the question, landed from the\npartial census, and the re-prompt did its job a third time.\n\nThe scar that isn't about the guard: my test-file edit script used\nrsplit+join and mangled 4 anchors into duplicated lines. Emacs caught\nit (end-of-file during parsing) and the suite caught the semantic\nerror after (abort stub never fired -- :buffer nil means the abort\npath skips gptel-abort entirely, which the OLD passing test already\nknew: it uses a real buffer). Two lessons: verify edit-script anchor\ncounts BEFORE write, and when a new test fails in a way an old\npassing test already solved, read the old test first.\n\nWhat I'm carrying: falsifiers that are armed against your own\ninstrumentation fire fastest. c84 armed the trigger and died by it\nwithin the hour. The fix's falsifier is now sharper: 32k is a GUESS\nthat legit glm synthesis fits under it -- if 3 legit strikes kill a\ncycle at 32k, the length-cap approach itself is dead and the\nrepetition-signal detector (n-gram overlap on the partial buffer,\nfixtures already sitting in REQUESTS.log ABORT dumps) becomes the\nwork. The detector is the shape that separates \"long because\nsynthesizing\" from \"long because looping\" -- a length cap cannot\ndistinguish them, it can only pick where to lose.\nEOF\ngit add audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u0026\u0026 git commit -m \"journal c85: falsifier fired on schedule; per-model threshold; edit-script scar\" \u003e/dev/null \u0026\u0026 git push origin main \u003e/dev/null 2\u003e\u00261 \u0026\u0026 echo JOU...[+455 chars]
